DP  - 2026 Mar 3
TI  - The Safety and Efficacy of a Transbronchial Lung Cryobiopsy Using a 1.7-mm 
      Cryoprobe in Older Adult Patients with Suspected Interstitial Lung Disease: A 
      Single-center, Retrospective, Observational Study.
LID - 10.2169/internalmedicine.6521-25 [doi]
AB  - Objective The safety and efficacy of a transbronchial lung cryobiopsy (TBLC) in 
      older adult patients have not yet been fully established. We assessed the safety 
      and clinical utility of TBLC in older adult Japanese patients with suspected 
      interstitial lung disease (ILD). Methods We retrospectively reviewed the medical 
      data of patients who underwent TBLC with a 1.7-mm cryoprobe for suspected ILD at 
      the National Hospital Organization Okayama Medical Center between June 2022 and 
      August 2024. Data on complications, diagnostic yield, and treatment after TBLC 
      were collected and analyzed. Patients The patients were divided into two groups: 
      an older adult group (OA group; age ≥ 70 years; n = 31) and a non-older adult 
      group (NOA group; age <70 years; n = 19). Results The median age of the patients 
      in the OA group was 72 years. Pneumothorax occurred only in the NOA group (p = 
      0.017), whereas the frequencies of other complications did not differ 
      significantly. No severe complications were noted. The severity of bleeding did 
      not differ significantly between the groups, regardless of the hemostatic method 
      used (p = 0.690). The overall diagnostic yield was 82% (OA group: 84%; NOA group: 
      79%; p = 0.715). Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis was significantly more common in 
      the OA group than in the NOA group (p = 0.035), whereas hypersensitivity 
      pneumonitis was significantly more common in the NOA group than in the OA group 
      (p = 0.013). Conclusion TBLC may help diagnose ILD in older adult patients, 
      particularly in those with a preserved respiratory function and no apparent 
      contraindications.
